1. First, defaults are set for ALL alias names of each option:
```typescript
for (const cliOption of cliOptions) {
  if (!ignoreDefault && cliOption.config.default !== undefined) {
    for (const name of cliOption.names) {
      options[name] = cliOption.config.default
    }
  }
}
```

2. Then, parsed values are set using only the camelCased name:
```typescript
for (const key of Object.keys(parsed)) {
  if (key !== '_') {
    const keys = key.split('.')
    setDotProp(options, keys, parsed[key])
    setByType(options, transforms)
  }
}
```

The problem is that when `--base-url` is parsed, it gets camelCased to `baseUrl`, and only `options.baseUrl` is updated. But `options.b` still has the default value.

The fix should be: When we set a parsed option value, we should also set all other alias names of that option to the same value.

Here's my fix:

```typescript
// Set option values (support dot-nested property name)
for (const key of Object.keys(parsed)) {
  if (key !== '_') {
    const keys = key.split('.')
    setDotProp(options, keys, parsed[key])
    setByType(options, transforms)

    // Set all alias names of the parsed option to the same value
    const option = cliOptions.find(o => o.names.includes(keys[0]))
    if (option) {
      for (const name of option.names) {
        if (name !== keys[0]) {
          options[name] = parsed[key]
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

Wait, but there's also a `setByType` call. Let me check if that needs to be applied to all aliases too.


Looking at `setByType`, it only transforms values based on the exact key passed to it, so it won't automatically propagate to other aliases. I need to handle both the value assignment and the type transformation for each alias of a parsed option.

